    "https://manmetjobs.mmu.ac.uk/jobs/vacancy/senior-research-assistant-child-and-adolescent-mental-health--9651-manchester-birley-campus/9547/description/": {
      "title": "Senior Research Assistant: Child and Adolescent Mental Health -",
      "description": "Senior Research Assistant: Child and Adolescent Mental Health  - (9651) | Manchester Metropolitan University Careers CareerZone - Senior Research Assistant: Child and Adolescent Mental Health  (9651) Open toolbar Accessibility Grayscale High contrast Negative contrast Light background Links underline Readable font Reset Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Senior Research Assistant: Child and Adolescent Mental Health  - (9651) We are looking for an enthusiastic Research Assistant to join the Wirral Child Health and Development Study (WCHADS/First Steps), a long-running longitudinal study following families from pregnancy through childhood and adolescence. You will join WCHADS at an exciting stage of its development, working on a new Wellcome-funded multi-cohort study examining how changes in social media policy affect young people's mental health and wellbeing. The project will recruit and follow approximately 550 young people aged 9–17 years and their families. You will take a central role in recruiting and retaining families, communicating with young people and parents, coordinating repeated questionnaire assessments, and responding sensitively to participant queries and concerns. You will also contribute to the development and piloting of age-appropriate psychological measures and study materials and co-facilitate a young person advisory group, ensuring young people's perspectives help shape the research. The role would particularly suit someone with an interest in child and adolescent mental health and developmental or clinical psychology who would value experience working directly with young people and families within a large longitudinal research study. You will work alongside psychologists and researchers specialising in child and adolescent mental health and development and as part of a wider collaboration involving several leading UK longitudinal cohorts. We are looking for someone who is organised, approachable and confident communicating with young people and families, including about sensitive issues relating to mental health and wellbeing. Experience of working or conducting research with children, young people or families would be particularly valuable. The role offers flexible hybrid working. However, you will work as part of a team based across Manchester Metropolitan University and the University of Liverpool and will be expected to attend both locations when required. The role also involves regular early-evening telephone contact with participating families, with flexibility in working hours to accommodate this. Application & Assessment To apply, submit your CV and supporting statement outlining how best you met the criteria, via our online portal. Please note applications received without a supporting statement will not be considered. Assessment will include a task to be completed on the day (no advance prep needed) followed by a competency-based interview round, and interviews will be held in the w/c 16 November. For informal enquiries about this vacancy, please contact Nicky Wright on Nicky.Wright@mmu.ac.uk Further Information Manchester Metropolitan University fosters an inclusive culture of belonging that promotes equity and celebrates diversity. We value a diverse workforce for the innovation and diversity of thought it brings and welcome applications from all local and international communities, including Black, Asian, and Minority Ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, and LGBTQ+ individuals. We support a range of flexible working arrangements, including hybrid and tailored schedules, which can be discussed with your line manager. If you require reasonable adjustments during the recruitment process or in your role, please let us know so we can provide appropriate support. Our commitment to inclusivity includes mentoring programmes, accessibility resources, and professional development opportunities to empower and support underrepresented groups. Manchester Met is a Disability Confident Leader and, under this scheme, aims to offer an interview to disabled people who apply for the role and meet the essential criteria as listed in the attached Job Description for that vacancy. Documents Wellcome - Senior Research Assistant JD.pdf (132.73 KB) Apply Now Apply Now Details Location: Manchester Birley Campus Faculty / Function: Health & Education Salary: Grade 6 (£31,861to £35,302) Closing Date: 5 November 2026 Contract Type: Fixed Term Contract Length: 2 years / 30.09.2028 Contracted Hours per week: 35 Benefits 25 days leave + Christmas closure + Bank holidays (Extra 5 days after 5 years of service) Travel loan Wellbeing platform Life insurance Access to Campus Facilities Enhanced parental leave High-quality guaranteed pension Meet the recruiter Neha Phanse ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk Loading Start at the next stage of your career Find out more about our opportunities, or get in touch to discuss bringing your expertise to a university where you'll be supported and valued. Explore our vacancies Get in touch Get in touch Manchester Metropolitan University All Saints Building Manchester M15 6BH United Kingdom ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk +44 (0)161 247 6820 Facebook icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Facebook Twitter icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Twitter YouTube icon Manchester Metropolitan University on YouTube LinkedIn icon Manchester Metropolitan University on LinkedIn Instagram icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Instagram Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply About us Search Get in touch Policies, regulations and legal notices Cookies Website terms of use Accessibility Freedom of information Governance of the University Modern slavery © 2026 Manchester Metropolitan University, all rights reserved."
    },
    "https://manmetjobs.mmu.ac.uk/jobs/vacancy/senior-identity--access-engineer--9639-manchester-all-saints-campus/9535/description/": {
      "title": "Senior Identity & Access Engineer",
      "description": "Senior Identity & Access Engineer  - (9639) | Manchester Metropolitan University Careers CareerZone - Senior Identity & Access Engineer  (9639) Open toolbar Accessibility Grayscale High contrast Negative contrast Light background Links underline Readable font Reset Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Senior Identity & Access Engineer  - (9639) Shape secure, seamless digital access across a modern university! As IT&D, we help to create the University of the future by co-designing new ways of working, enhancing productivity, reducing complexity, supporting innovation, and providing the insights to drive continual improvement. Embracing a digital future in an evolving modern university, you will be working in partnership with our academic and Professional Services colleagues to deliver digital services that help the University achieve its strategic objectives in teaching and research, nationally & globally. To know more about our Directorate of IT&D, click here IT and Digital opportunities at Manchester Met | Manchester Metropolitan University (cannot embed link: IT and Digital opportunities at Manchester Met | Manchester Metropolitan University ) About the Department Within IT & Digital, Infrastructure & Operations supports the core technology services that underpin the University's digital environment. Identity and Access Management is central to that foundation: it helps ensure the right people can access the right services, at the right time, while protecting University systems and information. This role combines specialist technical expertise with ownership of important service improvements and project deliverables. You'll help maintain business-critical identity services while shaping secure, scalable approaches that support the University's evolving needs. About the Team You'll report to the Hybrid Cloud Infrastructure Technical Lead and work closely with infrastructure engineers, colleagues across IT & Digital, the Service Management Office, and application business and technical owners. Success depends on sharing expertise, building trusted relationships and bringing people together to solve complex identity challenges. About the Role This is an opportunity for an experienced Identity and Access Management (IDAM) specialist to take a technical lead on workstreams that matter. You'll translate business and technical requirements into secure, scalable designs; guide the delivery of identity-related work packages within strategic projects; and continually improve the services that enable staff and students to work, learn and collaborate. You'll work across identity provider and Identity Governance and Administration (IGA) services, helping strengthen security, resilience and the user lifecycle experience. From developing technical standards and role-based access models to testing integrations, monitoring services and managing risks, you'll combine hands-on engineering with collaborative technical leadership. Key Responsibilities Lead IDAM design activities and technical work packages for strategic projects and continuous improvement initiatives, taking ownership of plans, deliverables, quality and timescales. Develop secure, scalable technical designs that translate University requirements into effective identity solutions and align with enterprise and solution architectures. Contribute to identity provider and Identity Governance and Administration (IGA) services, strengthening the security and resilience of University systems. Develop and promote technical patterns, standards and guidance, including Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) policies, models and guidelines aligned with University needs and security best practice. Review and improve identity and user lifecycle management processes to support a secure, consistent and positive user experience. Plan and execute end-to-end test plans and test cases to verify IDAM tools, systems and integrations; maintain clear, appropriate technical documentation. Proactively monitor identity infrastructure, maintain service documentation and identify issues before they affect students or staff. Build effective relationships with stakeholders and application owners, clarify requirements, manage expectations and communicate progress, risks and issues in a timely way. Support service enhancement and ITIL-aligned Incident, Problem and Change Management, working with the Service Management Office to improve service delivery and process integration. Horizon-scan identity and security trends, assess emerging technologies and make informed recommendations to senior colleagues. Key Skills & Experiences We're looking for someone who can combine deep technical knowledge with the confidence to lead complex workstreams and bring stakeholders with them. Extensive knowledge and practical experience of IDAM tools and principles, such as single sign-on (SSO), federation, multi-factor authentication (MFA), OpenLDAP, Shibboleth, Active Directory, identity lifecycle management, authentication and authorisation, RBAC, privileged access management (PAM), risk-based authentication and access reviews. Experience providing technical direction on strategic, large or complex security and/or identity and access management projects. A track record of leading technical design activities and taking responsibility for IDAM solutions that are compatible with enterprise and solution architectures. Ability to work with technical and non-technical stakeholders to understand requirements, manage expectations and keep people informed of progress. Strong identity and security fundamentals, with a structured approach to design, documentation, testing, risk management and continuous improvement. Experience working in a structured IT service management environment, including incident, change and problem management. Ability to select and apply appropriate design methods and tools, using plan-driven or iterative/agile approaches as appropriate. A customer-focused approach, strong interpersonal skills and the ability to establish productive relationships across diverse teams. To learn more about this exciting opportunity and benefits we offer, please read the JD and Candidate Pack provided below. Application & Assessment To apply, submit your CV and supporting statement outlining how best you met the criteria, via our online portal. Please note applications received without a supporting statement will not be considered. Assessment will include a competency-based interview round, and interviews will be held in the w/c 23 November. Due to the volume of applications, we receive, we sometimes close our vacancies early. It is therefore advisable to apply as early as possible if you would like to be considered for a role. For informal enquiries about this vacancy, please contact Ian Rushworth on i.rushworth@mmu.ac.uk If you would like to join our people and share our ambition, we would love to hear from you! Further information Manchester Metropolitan University fosters an inclusive culture of belonging that promotes equity and celebrates diversity. We value a diverse workforce for the innovation and diversity of thought it brings and welcome applications from all local and international communities, including Black, Asian, and Minority Ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, and LGBTQ+ individuals. We support a range of flexible working arrangements, including hybrid and tailored schedules, which can be discussed with your line manager. If you require reasonable adjustments during the recruitment process or in your role, please let us know so we can provide appropriate support. Our commitment to inclusivity includes mentoring programmes, accessibility resources, and professional development opportunities to empower and support underrepresented groups. Manchester Met is a Disability Confident Leader and, under this scheme, aims to offer an interview to disabled people who apply for the role and meet the essential criteria as listed in the attached Job Description for that vacancy. This vacancy is expected to attract a high volume of applications. Therefore, under the Disability Confident Scheme, only disabled people who apply under the scheme and best meet the essential criteria will be offered an interview. Documents Senior Identity and Access Engineer - JD.pdf (88.41 KB) Candidate Pack.pdf (9.15 MB) Apply Now Apply Now Details Location: Manchester All Saints Campus Faculty / Function: IT & Digital Salary: Competitive Closing Date: 8 November 2026 Contract Type: Permanent Contracted Hours per week: 37 Benefits 35 days leave + Christmas closure + Bank holidays Travel loan Wellbeing platform Life insurance Access to Campus Facilities Enhanced parental leave High-quality guaranteed pension Hybrid working opportunities Meet the recruiter Neha Phanse ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk Loading Start at the next stage of your career Find out more about our opportunities, or get in touch to discuss bringing your expertise to a university where you'll be supported and valued. Explore our vacancies Get in touch Get in touch Manchester Metropolitan University All Saints Building Manchester M15 6BH United Kingdom ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk +44 (0)161 247 6820 Facebook icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Facebook Twitter icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Twitter YouTube icon Manchester Metropolitan University on YouTube LinkedIn icon Manchester Metropolitan University on LinkedIn Instagram icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Instagram Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply About us Search Get in touch Policies, regulations and legal notices Cookies Website terms of use Accessibility Freedom of information Governance of the University Modern slavery © 2026 Manchester Metropolitan University, all rights reserved. Cookie Preferences This site uses cookies and other tracking technologies to improve your browsing experience, show personalised content/ads and analyse site traffic. Select cookies to accept Required cookies These cookies are used to ensure the user has a secure journey through the site. earcusessionid - Used to maintain your session. earcusession - Used to secure your session. earcucc - Used to manage your cookie preferences. Analytics Cookies Analytics cookies or performance cookies are used to track website visitors and their user behaviour. _ga - Used by Google Analytics. _gid - Used by Google Analytics. _gat - Used by Google Analytics. _fbp - Used by Meta (Facebook) Pixel. Accept Cookies Accept All Cookies Customise Cookies Reject All Optional"
