    "https://manmetjobs.mmu.ac.uk/jobs/vacancy/senior-research-associate-9586-manchester-birley-campus/9482/description/": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate -",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate - (9586) | Manchester Metropolitan University Careers CareerZone - Senior Research Associate (9586) Open toolbar Accessibility Grayscale High contrast Negative contrast Light background Links underline Readable font Reset Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Senior Research Associate - (9586) We are seeking an ambitious Senior Research Associate to join a multidisciplinary programme of research investigating Parkinson's disease, treatment response and deep brain stimulation (DBS). The postholder will contribute to a new clinical neuroscience study involving people with Parkinson's disease undergoing assessment for and treatment with DBS. The research uses a combination of digital measures of motor function, neuroimaging and electrophysiology , including recordings of brain activity from implanted DBS systems. Working closely with academic and NHS clinical collaborators, you will contribute to participant-facing research, acquisition and analysis of multimodal neuroscience data, and the development of biomarkers relevant to Parkinson's disease and treatment response. About the role You will: contribute to the delivery and coordination of a longitudinal research study involving people with Parkinson's disease; undertake and support research assessments and multimodal data collection; process and analyse complex neuroscience datasets, including EEG and intracranial/depth electrophysiological recordings ; contribute to analysis of neuroimaging, digital and clinical measures; develop robust and reproducible analytical approaches for multimodal human neuroscience data; work closely with NHS, academic and other external collaborators; prepare research findings for publication in high-quality peer-reviewed journals and presentation at national and international conferences; contribute to supervision and training of students and junior researchers; contribute to the development of future research questions and funding applications. About you You will have a PhD, or equivalent research experience, in neuroscience, psychology, biomedical engineering, neuroimaging, computational neuroscience or a related discipline, with significant experience of quantitative research. We particularly welcome applicants with expertise in electrophysiology, neural signal processing, neuroimaging, DBS, movement disorders or multimodal neuroscience . Previous experience across all of these areas is not required, but strong quantitative skills and experience of analysing complex biological or neural time-series data would be particularly valuable. You will be able to work independently, use initiative in developing analytical approaches, communicate complex research clearly, and work effectively within a multidisciplinary academic and clinical team. Research Environment The successful applicant will join a growing programme of translational neuroscience and Parkinson's disease research at Manchester Metropolitan University, with strong links to NHS DBS services and academic collaborators. The post offers an opportunity to develop expertise across human electrophysiology, deep brain stimulation, neuroimaging and digital neuroscience , while contributing to clinically relevant research in people with Parkinson's disease. Application & Assessment To apply, submit your CV and supporting statement outlining how best you met the criteria, via our online portal. Please note applications received without a supporting statement will not be considered. Assessment will include a presentation and a competency-based interview round. For informal enquiries about this vacancy, please contact Prof Nelson Trujillo Barreto on N.Trujillo.Barreto@mmu.ac.uk or Prof Nicola Ray on N.Ray@mmu.ac.uk Further Information Manchester Metropolitan University fosters an inclusive culture of belonging that promotes equity and celebrates diversity. We value a diverse workforce for the innovation and diversity of thought it brings and welcome applications from all local and international communities, including Black, Asian, and Minority Ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, and LGBTQ+ individuals. We support a range of flexible working arrangements, including hybrid and tailored schedules, which can be discussed with your line manager. If you require reasonable adjustments during the recruitment process or in your role, please let us know so we can provide appropriate support. Our commitment to inclusivity includes mentoring programmes, accessibility resources, and professional development opportunities to empower and support underrepresented groups. Manchester Met is a Disability Confident Leader and, under this scheme, aims to offer an interview to disabled people who apply for the role and meet the essential criteria as listed in the attached Job Description for that vacancy. Documents 000007.1 Research Associate Grade 8.pdf (73.51 KB) Apply Now Apply Now Details Location: Manchester Birley Campus Faculty / Function: Health & Education Salary: Grade 8 (£41,885 to £48,337) Closing Date: 8 November 2026 Contract Type: Fixed Term Contract Length: 3 years, October 12 2029 Contracted Hours per week: 35 Benefits 35 days leave + Christmas closure + Bank holidays Travel loan Wellbeing platform Life insurance Access to Campus Facilities Enhanced parental leave High-quality guaranteed pension Meet the recruiter Neha Phanse ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk Loading Start at the next stage of your career Find out more about our opportunities, or get in touch to discuss bringing your expertise to a university where you'll be supported and valued. Explore our vacancies Get in touch Get in touch Manchester Metropolitan University All Saints Building Manchester M15 6BH United Kingdom ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk +44 (0)161 247 6820 Facebook icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Facebook Twitter icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Twitter YouTube icon Manchester Metropolitan University on YouTube LinkedIn icon Manchester Metropolitan University on LinkedIn Instagram icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Instagram Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply About us Search Get in touch Policies, regulations and legal notices Cookies Website terms of use Accessibility Freedom of information Governance of the University Modern slavery © 2026 Manchester Metropolitan University, all rights reserved."
    },
    "https://manmetjobs.mmu.ac.uk/jobs/vacancy/senior-research-associate-in-computer-vision-and-3d-reconstruction-for-wildlife-conservation-9503-manchester-all-saints-campus/9400/description/": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate in Computer Vision and 3D Reconstruction for Wildlife Conservation -",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate in Computer Vision and 3D Reconstruction for Wildlife Conservation - (9503) | Manchester Metropolitan University Careers CareerZone - Senior Research Associate in Computer Vision and 3D Reconstruction for Wildlife Conservation (9503) Open toolbar Accessibility Grayscale High contrast Negative contrast Light background Links underline Readable font Reset Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Senior Research Associate in Computer Vision and 3D Reconstruction for Wildlife Conservation - (9503) About the Faculty Manchester Metropolitan University is one of the UK's largest universities, with a strong commitment to interdisciplinary research addressing real-world challenges. Our newly established School of Biological and Chemical Sciences , within the Faculty of Science and Engineering, brings together expertise spanning biodiversity, zoology, ecology, physiology and the biomedical sciences. The University has growing strengths in artificial intelligence, data science and computational research, supported by its new Centre for Digital Data Research (CDDR) . Together, these strengths provide an exciting environment for research at the intersection of AI, biological sciences and conservation. About the School You will join the School's Biodiversity and Environmental Resilience research group, where researchers investigate animal biology, evolution, behaviour and conservation, and develop innovative approaches to understanding and protecting the natural world. Our research combines traditional biological expertise with emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence, advanced 3D imaging and computational modelling. You will benefit from access to specialist research facilities, including imaging equipment in the University's new £1m Centre for Digital Modelling and Analysis for Cultural Heritage (D-MACH), and opportunities to engage with the wider Species Biology and Conservation (SBAC) research community through seminars, collaborative activities and knowledge exchange. About the Project This position is funded by the Natural Environment Research Council (NERC) through Phase 1 of its Artificial Intelligence for Environmental Science (AI4ES) programme . The project, Using Camera Trap Images to Reconstruct Wildlife 3D Body Size and Condition , aims to develop innovative AI-based methods for measuring animal size, shape and body condition from standard camera-trap photographs. By combining monocular depth estimation with single-image 3D reconstruction, the project will develop new approaches to recovering quantitative, real-world measurements of wild animals without the need for physical handling. Working with Chester Zoo and the Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology , we will develop and validate these methods using advanced 3D imaging techniques, including LiDAR and photogrammetry, with the longer-term aim of supporting non-invasive wildlife health monitoring and conservation. The project is led by Dr Charlotte Brassey (Principal Investigator) , alongside Manchester Met colleagues Dr Selvino de Kort and Dr Bradley Cain , and external collaborators Dr Peng Wang (University of Surrey), Dr Lisa Holmes (Chester Zoo) and Dr Adam van Casteren (Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology) . About the Role & Team You will play a central role in the day-to-day technical development and delivery of the project, with responsibility for developing, testing and validating AI-based approaches to reconstructing animal body size and shape from camera-trap imagery. Your work will primarily involve computational research, including model development, dataset curation and quantitative analysis, alongside opportunities for practical data collection and validation at Chester Zoo. You will be encouraged to take ownership of your research, contribute to peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations, and help shape future research directions, including the development of a larger Phase 2 funding application. You will also become part of the wider Biodiversity and Ecosystem Resilience research community, with opportunities to participate in the Species Biology and Conservation (SBAC) seminar series and professional development activities. Key Responsibilities Benchmark and fine-tune state-of-the-art monocular depth-estimation models using camera-trap imagery. Develop and evaluate AI-based methods for single-image 3D reconstruction of animals, integrating metric depth information to recover body dimensions and shape in real-world units. Curate and manage colour, LiDAR, photogrammetry and 3D datasets. Develop robust, reproducible computational pipelines using Python and relevant deep-learning frameworks. Undertake quantitative validation, statistical analysis and evaluation of model performance, accuracy and uncertainty. Collaborate with project partners to undertake data collection and validation at Chester Zoo. Contribute to the development and release of open-source software, datasets and trained models. Prepare research findings for publication in peer-reviewed journals and presentation at national and international conferences. Contribute to project management, dissemination, stakeholder engagement and the development of follow-on funding applications, including Phase 2 of the NERC AI4ES programme. Key Skills & Experiences We are looking for a highly motivated researcher with strong computational skills and experience in computer vision, machine learning, AI-based image analysis and/or 3D reconstruction. We welcome applicants from a broad range of disciplinary backgrounds; previous experience in ecology or zoology is not required. This is an interdisciplinary project, and we recognise that relevant expertise can be developed through a variety of research backgrounds and career pathways. We therefore encourage applications from candidates who bring transferable skills and enthusiasm for the project, even if they do not meet every criterion listed below. Essential criteria: A PhD (or close to completion) in a relevant discipline, such as Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Robotics, Computational Biology, Neuroscience, Engineering or another quantitative or computational discipline. Strong programming skills in Python. Practical experience with deep-learning frameworks such as PyTorch, TensorFlow or equivalent. Research experience developing, adapting and/or evaluating computer-vision or machine-learning models. Experience of quantitative analysis and rigorous evaluation of computational methods. Evidence of making a demonstrable, independent contribution to research projects. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively within an interdisciplinary research team. Excellent organisational skills, attention to detail and the ability to manage research activities and deadlines. Ability to travel to Chester Zoo and undertake on-site data collection. To learn more about role and requirements, please read the Job Description provided below. If you would like to join our people and share our ambition, we would love to hear from you! Application & Assessment Process: To apply, please submit a CV and covering letter detailing your suitability, on the application portal. For informal enquiries, about this vacancy, please contact Dr Charlotte Brassey on c.brassey@mmu.ac.uk Please be aware that only applications including a cover letter may be considered at shortlisting stage. Assessment will include a competency-based interview and a practical task to be completed on the day. Further details of the task will be provided with the interview invitation. Interviews are expected to be held in the w/c 16 November We believe in working together, sharing knowledge and valuing everyone's contributions. Develop your skills, further your knowledge and be part of a team who are transforming lives, every single day! Further Information Manchester Metropolitan University fosters an inclusive culture of belonging that promotes equity and celebrates diversity. We value a diverse workforce for the innovation and diversity of thought it brings and welcome applications from local and international communities, including those from Black, Asian, and Minority Ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, and LGBTQ+ individuals. We support a range of flexible working arrangements, including hybrid and tailored schedules, which can be discussed with your line manager. If you require reasonable adjustments during the recruitment process or in your role, please let us know so we can provide appropriate support. Our commitment to inclusivity includes mentoring programmes, accessibility resources, and professional development opportunities to empower and support underrepresented groups. Manchester Met is a Disability Confident Leader and, under this scheme, aims to offer an interview to disabled people who apply for the role and meet the essential criteria as listed in the attached Job Description for that vacancy. This vacancy is expected to attract a high volume of applications. Therefore, under the Disability Confident Scheme, only disabled people who apply under the scheme and best meet the essential criteria will be offered an interview. Find out more about our story and explore our campus ! Documents Research Associate Grade - JD.pdf (71.78 KB) Apply Now Apply Now Details Location: Manchester All Saints Campus Faculty / Function: Science & Engineering Salary: Grade 8 (£41,885 to £48,337) Closing Date: 8 November 2026 Contract Type: Fixed Term Contract Length: 31st March 2028 Contracted Hours per week: 37 Benefits 35 days leave + Christmas closure + Bank holidays Travel loan Wellbeing platform Life insurance Access to Campus Facilities Enhanced parental leave High-quality guaranteed pension Meet the recruiter Neha Phanse ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk Loading Start at the next stage of your career Find out more about our opportunities, or get in touch to discuss bringing your expertise to a university where you'll be supported and valued. Explore our vacancies Get in touch Get in touch Manchester Metropolitan University All Saints Building Manchester M15 6BH United Kingdom ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk +44 (0)161 247 6820 Facebook icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Facebook Twitter icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Twitter YouTube icon Manchester Metropolitan University on YouTube LinkedIn icon Manchester Metropolitan University on LinkedIn Instagram icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Instagram Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply About us Search Get in touch Policies, regulations and legal notices Cookies Website terms of use Accessibility Freedom of information Governance of the University Modern slavery © 2026 Manchester Metropolitan University, all rights reserved."
    },
    "https://manmetjobs.mmu.ac.uk/jobs/vacancy/lecturer-senior-lecturer-in-speech-and-language-therapy--9611-manchester-birley-campus/9507/description/": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Speech and Language Therapy -",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Speech and Language Therapy  - (9611) | Manchester Metropolitan University Careers CareerZone - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Speech and Language Therapy  (9611) Open toolbar Accessibility Grayscale High contrast Negative contrast Light background Links underline Readable font Reset Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Speech and Language Therapy  - (9611) Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Speech and Language Therapy As one of the largest providers of allied health professionals in the region, we offer a diverse range of award-winning and innovative undergraduate and postgraduate degree courses. Our work is professionally recognised, accredited and respected. We are proud of our diverse and inclusive learning environment that encourages discussion, debate, shared learning, and interdisciplinary co-operation. Our innovative programmes reflect our research activity, which enriches and informs the student experience. About the role: The Department of Health Professions at Manchester Metropolitan University delivers Speech and Language Therapy programmes at under-graduate and pre-registration master's level. As one of the largest providers of allied health professionals in the region, we offer a diverse range of award-winning and innovative undergraduate and postgraduate degree courses. Our work is professionally recognised, accredited and respected. We are proud of our diverse and inclusive learning environment that encourages discussion, debate, shared learning, and interdisciplinary co-operation. Our innovative programmes reflect our research activity, which enriches and informs the student experience. Are you an experienced Speech and Language Therapist, with current and/or previous background in adult acquired care? This exciting opportunity is a permanent role, suitable for either a specialist clinician who wants to develop their career in an academic setting, or an existing academic who wants to join our outstanding team of experienced staff. This role involves: high-quality teaching, outstanding student support, robust assessment of theory and practice, other education focused duties across our two programmes. You will be supported by internal mentorship and staff development to enable you to succeed in this role. This post is offered on the Education, Pedagogy and Citizenship (EPC) career pathway. About you: We are looking for a HCPC Registered Speech and Language Therapist who has extensive knowledge and experience in their field and is keen to teach and support across our programmes. We are specifically looking for a practitioner who has a background in adult acquired care. Candidates will hold a good first degree plus a master's degree or have evidence of progression towards a masters. Candidates with extensive relevant clinical experience without a master's degree, willing to engage in level 7 study to complete a Post-graduate Certificate in Academic Practice, will also be considered. To apply: We are recruiting a full-time permanent Lecturer/Senior Lecturer (dependent on experience) to join our outstanding team. If you are looking for a new opportunity focused on excellent educational delivery and would like to join our ambitious team, we would love to hear from you. Please submit your CV and covering letter via the online portal. Further information: Closing date for applications: Monday 9 th November 2026 at 11.59pm Interviews are scheduled to take place on Friday 20th November 2026 at the Brooks Building, Birley Campus, M15 6GX If you wish to find out more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Neil Wilson on neil.wilson@mmu.ac.uk for an informal discussion. Manchester Met University is committed to creating an intentionally inclusive culture of belonging that promotes equity and celebrates diversity. We understand the importance of having a diverse workforce and the benefits it can bring to ensuring diversity of thought and innovation in everything we do. We, therefore, encourage applications from our local and international communities, in particular people from ethnic minority groups, disabled people and people who identify as LGBTQIA+ #LI-MP1 Documents Candidate Pack (2).pdf (9.15 MB) Lecturer JD EPC G8 SLT Oct 2026.pdf (277.59 KB) Senior Lecturer JD EPC SLT Oct 26.pdf (290.36 KB) Apply Now Apply Now Details Location: Manchester Birley Campus Faculty / Function: Health & Education Salary: Grade 8 (£41,885 - £48,337) Grade 9 (£49,798 - £57,666) Closing Date: 9 November 2026 Contract Type: Permanent Contracted Hours per week: 37 Benefits 35 days leave + Christmas closure + Bank holidays Travel loan Wellbeing platform Life insurance Access to Campus Facilities Enhanced parental leave High-quality guaranteed pension Meet the recruiter Neha Phanse ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk Loading Start at the next stage of your career Find out more about our opportunities, or get in touch to discuss bringing your expertise to a university where you'll be supported and valued. Explore our vacancies Get in touch Get in touch Manchester Metropolitan University All Saints Building Manchester M15 6BH United Kingdom ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk +44 (0)161 247 6820 Facebook icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Facebook Twitter icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Twitter YouTube icon Manchester Metropolitan University on YouTube LinkedIn icon Manchester Metropolitan University on LinkedIn Instagram icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Instagram Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply About us Search Get in touch Policies, regulations and legal notices Cookies Website terms of use Accessibility Freedom of information Governance of the University Modern slavery © 2026 Manchester Metropolitan University, all rights reserved."
    },
    "https://manmetjobs.mmu.ac.uk/jobs/vacancy/research-associate--the-emotional-life-of-clothes--9677-manchester-all-saints-campus/9573/description/": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate – The Emotional Life of Clothes  - (9677) | Manchester Metropolitan University Careers CareerZone - Research Associate – The Emotional Life of Clothes  (9677) Open toolbar Accessibility Grayscale High contrast Negative contrast Light background Links underline Readable font Reset Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Research Associate – The Emotional Life of Clothes  - (9677) About the Institute Manchester Fashion Institute , part of Manchester Metropolitan University, is a leading global fashion school spanning design, communication, business, technology and heritage, with recent investment in digital facilities, new academic appointments and a multimillion-pound fashion robotics lab. We value diversity and welcome applications from all backgrounds, particularly from groups underrepresented in higher education. Our research community is thriving, with fashion contributing to an Art & Design REF submission ranked second in the UK for research power; across the University, 90% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent. The successful candidate will become a visiting scholar within the F/fashion Narratives Research Group, with access to research training and career development opportunities at both Manchester Met. and the University of Manchester. About the Project Dr Benjamin Wild (Project Lead), with Professor Emma Banister (University of Manchester), Dr Jo Jenkinson and Jolene Sheehan (Joy Ethic) as Project Co-Leads, has secured ESRC funding for ‘The Emotional Life of Clothes', a 30-month project exploring how people's emotional connections with clothing can support circular fashion and sustainable lifestyles. Reframing people as ‘wearers' rather than ‘consumers', the project will build new knowledge to inform narrative and policy strategies for how we buy, repair, recycle and replace clothes. Focused on Greater Manchester, the UK's ‘fast-fashion capital', it brings together Manchester Met, the University of Manchester, community groups, fashion intermediaries, Greater Manchester Combined Authority (GMCA) and Heard Storytelling, with outputs feeding into GMCA policy and a national industry report. Further project details: https://fashionnarratives.mmu.ac.uk/the-emotional-life-of-clothes-leveraging-the-connection-between-emotion-and-clothing-to-support-circular-fashion-through-sustainable-lifestyles/ About the Role This is a rare opportunity to join the project as its sole full-time researcher, working across the entire research cycle from typology-building and community fieldwork through to co-authored publications and public dissemination. Managed by the Project Lead, you will play an integral role in theoretical framing, participant recruitment, data collection and management, and project outputs. The role is full time (35 hours per week) and fixed term for 29 months, with an anticipated start date on or before 31 December 2026. The role is based at Manchester Met, with hybrid/home working available on days without in-person commitments (e.g. fieldwork, training, meetings and engagement activities). Evening and weekend work is likely to be required, along with regular travel across Greater Manchester and occasional travel further afield for advisory board meetings and conferences. Key Responsibilities Help build a typology of narratives about emotion and clothing consumption, contributing to the project's theoretical framing. Recruit participants, and co-lead and help coordinate storytelling workshops with c.8 Greater Manchester community groups. Co-develop a creative toolkit. Co-develop and run focus groups exploring circular fashion policy. Manage research data securely, including transcription and anonymisation, in line with data protection and research ethics requirements. Act as lead or co-author on peer-reviewed journal articles. Contribute to public storytelling events, community dissemination workshops and an industry-facing forum. Set up and run the project's social media presence. Key Skills & Experiences We are looking for someone with a good understanding of, and genuine interest in, emotion, narrative and clothing consumption behaviour, ideally with an interest in circular fashion and sustainability. Essential A doctoral qualification (held or nearing completion) in a relevant social science or humanities discipline (e.g. Sociology, Fashion/Cultural Studies, Human Geography or Marketing/Consumer Research). Qualitative research skills, including interviewing, workshop or focus-group facilitation, and narrative or thematic analysis. Experience of, or willingness to be trained in, narrative inquiry methods. Ability to build trust and work sensitively with a wide range of community groups, including vulnerable participants, and to maintain respectful relationships with group leads and members. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to coordinate concurrent workstreams (workshops, focus groups, transcription and toolkit production) to deadline. Confidence handling sensitive personal data securely and in line with data protection and research ethics requirements. Strong academic writing skills, with the ability to co-author peer-reviewed journal articles. Confidence engaging with stakeholders across academia, community organisations, local government and the fashion industry, and communicating with academic and non-academic audiences. Comfortable setting up and maintaining a project website and Instagram presence. A self-starter, able to work independently and across two institutions. Willingness to undertake safeguarding training and to take part in academic meetings and networks that extend the reach of the research. Desirable Experience of community-based or participatory research with diverse or underrepresented groups. Experience of public engagement outputs, such as podcasts, blogs, live storytelling events or exhibitions. Experience of working with local government, combined authorities or policy stakeholders. Familiarity with institutional research data management systems and FAIR data principles. To learn more about this exciting opportunity and benefits we offer, please read the JD provided below. Application & Assessment To apply, submit your CV and supporting statement outlining how best you met the criteria, via our online portal. Please note applications received without a supporting statement will not be considered. Assessment will include a competency-based interview round. The interview date is Friday 27 November 2026. As part of the interview, candidates will be presented with a research scenario and asked to develop a brief qualitative research plan to assess their creative and practical research design skills. For informal enquiries about this vacancy, please contact Dr Benjamin Wild on b.wild@mmu.ac.uk If you would like to join our people and share our ambition, we would love to hear from you! Further Information Manchester Metropolitan University fosters an inclusive culture of belonging that promotes equity and celebrates diversity. We value a diverse workforce for the innovation and diversity of thought it brings and welcome applications from local and international communities, including those from Black, Asian, and Minority Ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, and LGBTQ+ individuals. We support a range of flexible working arrangements, including hybrid and tailored schedules, which can be discussed with your line manager. If you require reasonable adjustments during the recruitment process or in your role, please let us know so we can provide appropriate support. Our commitment to inclusivity includes mentoring programmes, accessibility resources, and professional development opportunities to empower and support underrepresented groups. Manchester Met is a Disability Confident Leader and, under this scheme, aims to offer an interview to disabled people who apply for the role and meet the essential criteria as listed in the attached Job Description for that vacancy. Documents Job Description_RIA_ELC.pdf (90.38 KB) Apply Now Apply Now Details Location: Manchester All Saints Campus Faculty / Function: Arts & Humanities Salary: Grade 7 (£36,320 to £40,704) Closing Date: 16 November 2026 Contract Type: Fixed Term Contract Length: 29 months/30 April 2029 Contracted Hours per week: 35 Benefits 25 days leave + Christmas closure + Bank holidays (Extra 5 days after 5 years of service) Travel loan Wellbeing platform Life insurance Access to Campus Facilities Enhanced parental leave High-quality guaranteed pension Meet the recruiter Neha Phanse ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk Loading Start at the next stage of your career Find out more about our opportunities, or get in touch to discuss bringing your expertise to a university where you'll be supported and valued. Explore our vacancies Get in touch Get in touch Manchester Metropolitan University All Saints Building Manchester M15 6BH United Kingdom ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk +44 (0)161 247 6820 Facebook icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Facebook Twitter icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Twitter YouTube icon Manchester Metropolitan University on YouTube LinkedIn icon Manchester Metropolitan University on LinkedIn Instagram icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Instagram Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply About us Search Get in touch Policies, regulations and legal notices Cookies Website terms of use Accessibility Freedom of information Governance of the University Modern slavery © 2026 Manchester Metropolitan University, all rights reserved."
    },
    "https://manmetjobs.mmu.ac.uk/jobs/vacancy/technical-services-health--safety-officer-9594-manchester-all-saints-campus/9490/description/": {
      "title": "Technical Services Health & Safety Officer",
      "description": "Technical Services Health & Safety Officer - (9594) | Manchester Metropolitan University Careers CareerZone - Technical Services Health & Safety Officer (9594) Open toolbar Accessibility Grayscale High contrast Negative contrast Light background Links underline Readable font Reset Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply Professorial opportunities Login Technical Services Health & Safety Officer - (9594) We are seeking an enthusiastic and proactive Health and Safety Officer to join our team in Technical Services! How to apply: To apply, please click \"Apply Now\" and complete the online application form. Please upload: an up-to-date CV and a Cover Letter outlining your suitability for the role In your cover letter (max. 2 pages), please refer to the Job Description and Person Specification (attached below), providing clear examples of how your skills, knowledge and experience meet the essential criteria and, where applicable, the desirable criteria. This will help the shortlisting panel to fully assess your application against the requirements of the role. About our Team: Technical Services provides on campus specialist support for learning, teaching, research and enterprise activities across Manchester Metropolitan University. Working across laboratories, workshops, studios, maker spaces, production environments and specialist facilities, our teams enable innovative teaching and research across a wide range of disciplines. The Technical Services Health & Safety Team works in partnership with technical and academic colleagues to promote safe, compliant and well-managed working environments. Through practical face-to-face support, expert guidance and continuous improvement, the team helps ensure Technical Services activities are delivered safely, sustainably and in accordance with the University and legislative requirements. About the Role: This is an exciting opportunity to join the Technical Services as a Health & Safety Officer. Reporting to the Technical Services Health & Safety Manager, you will provide operational support in maintaining safe, compliant and well-managed technical environments across the University. Working closely with Technical Managers, Technical Officers, academic colleagues and professional services teams, you will play a key role in supporting health and safety compliance across laboratories, workshops, studios, production spaces and specialist facilities. The role combines: practical health and safety support, inspections, risk management, incident investigation, training and record keeping. You will help ensure that safety systems, documentation and compliance activities are effectively implemented, maintained and continuously improved. Please refer to the attached Job Description for full details of the duties and responsibilities associated with this role. About You: We are looking for a proactive and organised individual who is passionate about health and safety and enjoys working collaboratively with a wide range of stakeholders in technical environments. You will be able to demonstrate: Experience working within a laboratory, workshop, studio, fabrication, digital, manufacturing or other technical environment. Experience supporting risk assessment processes and incident investigations. Knowledge of current UK health and safety legislation and its practical application. Excellent communication skills, with the ability to explain health and safety requirements clearly and effectively. Strong organisational skills and attention to detail, particularly in record keeping and compliance management. Ability to work independently and collaboratively while providing excellent customer service. Proficiency in Microsoft Office 365 applications, including Outlook, Word, Excel and SharePoint. A NEBOSH General Certificate qualification. A degree in a STEM discipline, or equivalent relevant experience. Desirable Experience working within a higher education or research environment. Experience of fume cupboard monitoring, local exhaust ventilation (LEV) systems and face velocity testing. Knowledge of specialist technical environments and associated hazards. Why Join Us? Be part of a skilled and supportive Technical Services team. Join a dedicated Technical Services Health & Safety Team supporting a diverse range of specialist facilities. Play a key role in enabling safe teaching, research and innovation activities across the University. Develop your health and safety expertise across laboratories, workshops, and studios. Access professional development opportunities and ongoing training. Work within a collaborative, supportive and values-led community committed to continuous improvement. Benefit from a comprehensive range of wellbeing initiatives, staff networks and colleague benefits. Access training and development opportunities to broaden your technical skills and support your career progression. Join a department focused on collaboration, service excellence and continuous improvement. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and values-led university community where your contribution is valued. Access a range of wellbeing support initiatives, staff networks and colleague benefits. Work for a university committed to making a positive impact through outstanding education, research and innovation. Further Information This role is campus-based and requires flexibility to work across University sites. The post holder will be required to work flexibly, with occasional evening or weekend working to support operational requirements. This role will suit someone that enjoys working face-to-face with people on campus. To learn more about what it's like to work at Manchester Met and the full range of colleague benefits available, please see the attached Candidate Pack below. To be considered for this role, please click \"Apply Now\" and complete the online application form. You will be required to upload your CV and a cover letter For an informal discussion about the role, please contact Naomi Bates, Technical Services Health and Safety Manager, N.Bates@mmu.ac.uk . Please note: Interviews are provisionally scheduled for the week commencing 14 December 2026. Candidates should ensure they are available to attend on this week. Manchester Metropolitan University fosters an inclusive culture of belonging that promotes equity and celebrates diversity. We value a diverse workforce for the innovation and diversity of thought it brings and welcome applications from all local and international communities, including Black, Asian, and Minority Ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, and LGBTQ+ individuals. If you require reasonable adjustments during the recruitment process or in your role, please let us know so we can provide appropriate support. Our commitment to inclusivity includes mentoring programmes, accessibility resources, and professional development opportunities to empower and support underrepresented groups. Manchester Met is a Disability Confident Leader and, under this scheme, aims to offer an interview to disabled people who apply for the role and meet the essential criteria as listed in the attached Job Description for that vacancy. Documents 000208.1 Technical Services Health & Safety Officer.pdf (89.62 KB) Candidate Pack.pdf (9.16 MB) Apply Now Apply Now Details Location: Manchester All Saints Campus Faculty / Function: Academic Services Salary: Grade 6 (£31,236 to £34,610) Closing Date: 22 November 2026 Contract Type: Permanent Contracted Hours per week: 35 Benefits 25 days leave + Christmas closure + Bank holidays (Extra 5 days after 5 years of service) Travel loan Wellbeing platform Life insurance Access to Campus Facilities Enhanced parental leave High-quality guaranteed pension Meet the recruiter Claire Muir ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk Loading Start at the next stage of your career Find out more about our opportunities, or get in touch to discuss bringing your expertise to a university where you'll be supported and valued. Explore our vacancies Get in touch Get in touch Manchester Metropolitan University All Saints Building Manchester M15 6BH United Kingdom ManMetJobs@mmu.ac.uk +44 (0)161 247 6820 Facebook icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Facebook Twitter icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Twitter YouTube icon Manchester Metropolitan University on YouTube LinkedIn icon Manchester Metropolitan University on LinkedIn Instagram icon Manchester Metropolitan University on Instagram Explore vacancies Working at Manchester Met Living in Manchester How to apply About us Search Get in touch Policies, regulations and legal notices Cookies Website terms of use Accessibility Freedom of information Governance of the University Modern slavery © 2026 Manchester Metropolitan University, all rights reserved. Cookie Preferences This site uses cookies and other tracking technologies to improve your browsing experience, show personalised content/ads and analyse site traffic. Select cookies to accept Required cookies These cookies are used to ensure the user has a secure journey through the site. earcusessionid - Used to maintain your session. earcusession - Used to secure your session. earcucc - Used to manage your cookie preferences. Analytics Cookies Analytics cookies or performance cookies are used to track website visitors and their user behaviour. _ga - Used by Google Analytics. _gid - Used by Google Analytics. _gat - Used by Google Analytics. _fbp - Used by Meta (Facebook) Pixel. Accept Cookies Accept All Cookies Customise Cookies Reject All Optional"
